# This host runs Ubuntu's -server NVIDIA driver. The same driver's non-server
# packages conflict with it, and installing one removes the -server userspace.
# Their plain names are easy to reach by accident: libnvidia-encode-580 is the
# non-server build, and NVIDIA's CUDA repository, pinned at 600 by
# cuda-repository-pin-600, makes its own build of each one the default
# candidate. That repository also ships unversioned names (libnvidia-encode)
# and the nvidia-open and cuda-drivers metapackages, which pull in the same
# conflicting set.
#
# Priority -1 makes apt refuse every version from every origin. Every binary of
# the non-server driver, Ubuntu's or NVIDIA's, is built from source package
# nvidia-graphics-drivers or nvidia-graphics-drivers-NNN, and every -server
# binary from nvidia-graphics-drivers-NNN-server, so the src: pattern refuses
# the whole non-server family, firmware included, and leaves every -server
# package installable. The two name patterns cover the metapackages, which are
# built from sources of their own. nvidia-fabricmanager, libnvidia-nscq,
# libnvidia-egl-wayland1, libnvidia-container*, and nvidia-container-toolkit
# serve the -server driver under plain names and come from other sources.
#
# apt takes the first pin in file order that matches a package. NVIDIA's
# nvidia-driver-pinning-NNN package installs nvidia-driver-pin, which pins the
# non-server source at 1000; the 00- prefix sorts this file ahead of it.
#
# A host moving to the non-server driver removes this file first.
# Deployed by scripts/nvidia-deploy.sh; nvidia-gpu-verify checks that apt
# honours it.
Package: src:/^nvidia-graphics-drivers(-[0-9]+)?$/ /^nvidia-open(-[0-9]+)?$/ /^cuda-drivers(-[0-9]+)?$/
Pin: version *
Pin-Priority: -1
